{"timestamp": "2026-11-09T03:59:14.893869+00:00", "input": {"text": "I'd rather have you answer it, so thanks. My point is that if intent or knowledge is required, that means you must be aware of a crime by you or someone else. Assuming your property has remained under your control, and that you have committed no crime (which is always supposed to be the presumption), the chief way by which you would find out that your own property, that you can usually destroy freely, is now deemed \"evidence\" and not to be destroyed, is by being charged with a crime involving said property. Until you are charged, you don't know it's evidence. So the charge is not irrelevant. Anyone can read the damn law, I'm saying the law is bullshit and rests on circular reasoning. Charging with destroying evidence will in some cases require presuming guilt for the crime the evidence supposedly supports."}, "output": {"label": "argument", "confidence": 0.32852575182914734}, "status": "ok", "duration_ms": 2274.89, "cpu_seconds": 2.297668}
{"timestamp": "2026-11-09T03:59:17.168905+00:00", "input": {"text": "How do I get started though? :/ I feel like I need a couple mil in seed funding and a team that understands the mission."}, "output": {"label": "assertion", "confidence": 0.2763720452785492}, "status": "ok", "duration_ms": 39.89, "cpu_seconds": 0.068904}
{"timestamp": "2026-11-09T03:59:17.208983+00:00", "input": {"text": "Any model that is going to be meaningfully useful is going to have the potential for harm too. It's not possible to know enough about chemistry to be useful to a chemist, without also being able to figure out how to synthesize drugs. It's not possible to have enough knowledge to be useful to any/all programmers, without being able to figure out how to hack a system, or create a virus. That's just kind of life though."}, "output": {"label": "argument", "confidence": 0.29212096333503723}, "status": "ok", "duration_ms": 37.927, "cpu_seconds": 0.066206}
{"timestamp": "2026-11-09T03:59:17.247037+00:00", "input": {"text": "Its probably reached a certain level of intelligence but only operating in a very confined environment.\n\nAnd I assume heavily language based.\n\nHuman brains use language for communication and other things but it isnt the only part of intelligence.  There is also the intelligence of adapting and surviving in the world.\n\nUntil the AI can have a virtual environment or use the physical environment to interact with.  I am still not convinced."}, "output": {"label": "argument", "confidence": 0.2710903584957123}, "status": "ok", "duration_ms": 38.814, "cpu_seconds": 0.067012}
{"timestamp": "2026-11-09T03:59:17.285969+00:00", "input": {"text": "In 1992, I got myself an AT&T EasyReach 700 number. It was going to be my phone number for life. They killed that a couple years later. Then I got an AT&T True Connections 500 number. It was going to be my phone number for life. That died a few years later too."}, "output": {"label": "argument", "confidence": 0.2847762107849121}, "status": "ok", "duration_ms": 42.24, "cpu_seconds": 0.065709}
{"timestamp": "2026-11-09T03:59:17.328315+00:00", "input": {"text": "You can easily setup codex rules to auto approve local stuff but gate external effects like push/jira write/curl - works better for me than full yolo mode - depends if it's a solo project or working with a team."}, "output": {"label": "experience", "confidence": 0.2792125642299652}, "status": "ok", "duration_ms": 52.873, "cpu_seconds": 0.068975}
{"timestamp": "2026-11-09T03:59:17.381803+00:00", "input": {"text": "Worth checking out the methodology this guy put together using Jev and Haiku at appropriate points to cut the cost and also speed up a deep research job:\nhttps://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50019056"}, "output": {"label": "argument", "confidence": 0.2753179669380188}, "status": "ok", "duration_ms": 49.553, "cpu_seconds": 0.056565}
{"timestamp": "2026-11-09T03:59:17.431506+00:00", "input": {"text": "This would be a good strategy if there are other people in the waiting area handing out coupons for competing restaurants, which is what is happening in Amazon."}, "output": {"label": "assertion", "confidence": 0.28722289204597473}, "status": "ok", "duration_ms": 29.192, "cpu_seconds": 0.051707}
{"timestamp": "2026-11-09T03:59:17.460843+00:00", "input": {"text": "My estimate is very much anecdotal as well, but at my office alone there are three iPhone mini users than (used to be four until they switched job), only one AirPods Max user. I think this year I have only seen one other person wearing them. Much harder to see the exact model of phone people use without staring in a bad manner."}, "output": {"label": "argument", "confidence": 0.30688658356666565}, "status": "ok", "duration_ms": 27.965, "cpu_seconds": 0.052372}
{"timestamp": "2026-11-09T03:59:17.488897+00:00", "input": {"text": "One elevator feature I specifically noticed at Apple Park but haven't seen anywhere else: elevator prefetching to the ground floor.\n\nI.e. in a bank of 2 elevators where one is resting on ground and the other is resting on a different floor, as soon as someone calls the ground elevator (which is already there and immediately opens) the other elevator (if idle) will move to the ground floor so that there's no delay the next time someone calls one from the ground floor."}, "output": {"label": "argument", "confidence": 0.30801519751548767}, "status": "ok", "duration_ms": 30.156, "cpu_seconds": 0.055808}
